<main>
  <h1>prove.my</h1>
  <p><strong>An anonymizing KYC broker.</strong></p>
  <p>
    prove.my sits between the many government age and driving-licence services
    and the many operators that need to gate a regulated action. A human
    authorizes prove.my once; it returns to the requesting operator a
    <strong>signed, anonymized, single-use</strong> claim — "over 18",
    "holds a category-A licence" — and <strong>nothing that identifies the
    person</strong>: not the name, date of birth, or licence number.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each operator trusts prove.my as an issuer once and asks for exactly the
    facts it needs. The operator never registers with a government identity
    service — prove.my does that, once, for everyone.
  </p>

  <p class="stub">
    <strong>Demo stub — this does not check anything yet.</strong> On the
    verification page you simply <em>self-assert</em> the facts (you click
    yes/no); prove.my takes your word for it. The <strong>real</strong> broker
    does two things this stub does not: first it verifies you actually
    <strong>possess a government account</strong> — you log into a national
    eID or a mobile driving licence (mDL) and authorize prove.my to read your
    date of birth and licence category — and later that
    <strong>the account is genuinely yours</strong>, so it cannot be lent or
    shared to vouch for other people's age or licence. This demo proves the
    <em>protocol</em> (per-request binding, no replay, anti-mass-confirm,
    signed anonymized callback) — <strong>not</strong> that the human is
    actually over 18, and it makes no liveness claim.
  </p>

  <p class="stub">
    <strong>Not a Kiosk operator.</strong> prove.my is an <em>issuer</em>, not
    a Kiosk site: it exposes none of the four Kiosk verbs, has no agent
    endpoint, and serves no discovery document. This is a page for a human,
    reached from a link your assistant relays to you.
  </p>

  <p class="stub">
    Verifications in flight: <%= @pending %> pending, <%= @confirmed %> confirmed.
    The verification page is reached only from an operator-issued link.
  </p>
</main>
